<h1>Gravity Roller Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A gravity roller conveyor moves products using gravity on a slight decline or by manual push on a level surface, with no motors, belts, or external power source. Gravity roller conveyors are the lowest-cost, lowest-maintenance conveyor type available and handle flat-bottomed cases, cartons, and totes across a wide weight range depending on roller and frame construction. They serve as the default conveyor for loading docks, manual packing stations, shipping areas, and anywhere products need simple point-to-point transport without speed control or accumulation.</p>
<h2 id="how-heading">How Gravity Roller Conveyors Work</h2>
<p>Gravity roller conveyors operate on the simplest mechanical principle in material handling. Free-spinning rollers mounted on precision bearings rotate under the weight of the product. A slight downward angle converts gravitational potential energy into kinetic energy, moving the product forward without any power input. The rollers themselves provide minimal rolling resistance, allowing even lightweight products to travel significant distances on a modest decline.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/gravity-omni-roller-section.webp" width="1000" height="561" alt="A straight section of gravity roller conveyor with steel rollers set between two galvanized side channels, shown without legs." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A straight gravity roller conveyor section from Omni Metalcraft. Free-turning rollers sit on axles between two side channels, with no drive. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://omni.com/products/gravity-conveyor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Omni Metalcraft</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Decline Angle and Pitch</h3>
<p>The decline (also called pitch or slope) determines whether product rolls and how fast. Hytrol's <a href="https://www.cisco-eagle.com/uploads/Hytrol-Literature/Basic-Conveyor-Training.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Basic Training Manual</a> lists four factors: roller or skatewheel type, bearing lubrication (grease-packed bearings roll slower), package weight, and the firmness of the package bottom. Its starting chart, in inches of drop per 10 feet, includes:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Item</th>
<th>Approx. Weight</th>
<th>Slope per 10 ft</th>
<th>About per Foot</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Cartons</td>
<td>3-6 lbs</td>
<td>8-3/4 in.</td>
<td>7/8 in.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cartons</td>
<td>6-12 lbs</td>
<td>7-1/2 in.</td>
<td>3/4 in.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cartons</td>
<td>12-25 lbs</td>
<td>6-1/4 in.</td>
<td>5/8 in.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cartons</td>
<td>25-30 lbs</td>
<td>5 in.</td>
<td>1/2 in.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Tote pans</td>
<td>Not stated</td>
<td>2-1/2 to 5 in.</td>
<td>1/4 to 1/2 in.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Drums</td>
<td>150-300 lbs</td>
<td>2-1/2 to 3-3/4 in.</td>
<td>1/4 to 3/8 in.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Heavier products require less decline. Lighter products require more. Hytrol calls the chart a guideline and says to test the product, and to contact the factory before using free-running gravity for boxes over 200 lbs. The only reliable method to determine the correct pitch is testing with actual product on the specific roller conveyor being installed. Manufacturers typically ship gravity conveyors with adjustable-height legs so the installer can fine-tune the decline angle on site.</p>
<h3>Roller Diameter Selection</h3>
<p>Roller diameter, wall thickness, and axle size set the load per roller. Omni Metalcraft's <a href="https://omni.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/Gravity_Conveyor_SPC-1.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">gravity conveyor catalog section</a> gives one manufacturer's ratings:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>1.4" diameter</strong>, 18-gauge tube, 1/4" round axle: 94 lbs maximum per roller. Light cartons, small parts, and poly bags.</li>
<li><strong>1.9" diameter</strong>, 16, 13, or 9-gauge tube, 7/16" hex axle: 269 lbs maximum per roller. The most common size for cases, cartons, and totes.</li>
<li><strong>2.5" diameter</strong>, 11-gauge tube (7-gauge on its 2.6" roller), 11/16" hex axle: 645 lbs maximum per roller. Heavy totes, bins, and drums.</li>
</ul>
<p>Product weight is shared by the rollers under it, so a product resting on several rollers can weigh more than one roller's rating. Uneven bottoms, drop loading, and impact reduce that margin. Ask for the per-roller rating and the frame rating for your exact configuration.</p>
<h3>Roller Spacing Rules</h3>
<p>Roller pitch (center-to-center spacing) must keep at least three rollers under the product at all times. Hytrol's training manual and Ashland's <a href="https://www.ashlandconveyor.com/pages/roller-conveyor" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">roller conveyor selection guide</a> both state this rule. With fewer rollers, boxes rock, tumble, or jam between rollers. The formula is simple:</p>
<p class="formula"><strong>Maximum roller pitch = smallest product length / 3</strong></p>
<p>Available centers vary by roller size. Omni Metalcraft lists 1.5, 3, 4, 4.5, 6, 8, 9, and 12 inches for 1.4" rollers, and 2, 3, 4, 4.5, 6, 8, 9, and 12 inches for 1.9" rollers. A 12" case requires 4" pitch or smaller. A 24" carton allows 8" pitch or smaller. Closer pitch provides smoother transport and lower per-roller loading but increases cost per foot because more rollers are needed.</p>
<h3>Frame Construction</h3>
<p>Gravity roller conveyor frames use channel or angle side rails. Frame gauge rises with roller size. In Omni Metalcraft's catalog, 1.4" roller frames use 12-gauge galvanized steel or 1/8" aluminum, 1.9" roller frames use 10-gauge steel, and 2.5" roller frames use 7-gauge steel. Width is specified between frame rails (BF). Overall width adds the rail thickness and any guard rails.</p>
<p>Support leg options include fixed-height, adjustable tripod, and floor-mount H-stands. Adjustable legs allow on-site fine-tuning of the decline. Support spacing sets frame capacity: Omni Metalcraft rates its 1.9" steel frame at 3,300 lbs uniformly distributed on 5-foot support centers and 1,200 lbs on 10-foot centers.</p>
<h3>Curve Sections</h3>
<p>Gravity roller curves come in straight-roller, tapered-roller, skatewheel, and split-roller designs. Hytrol's training manual notes that boxes on tapered-roller curves keep better orientation, and that skatewheel and split-roller curves also keep boxes oriented. Curves often need to be wider than the straight sections so long package corners clear the guard rail. Larger radii give smoother flow and reduce jams.</p>
