<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What angle do you need for a gravity roller conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Hytrol's starting chart for cartons runs from 8-3/4 in. per 10 ft (3-6 lb cartons) down to 5 in. per 10 ft (25-30 lb cartons), about 7/8" to 1/2" per foot. Heavier products need less decline. Lighter products need more. Pitch also depends on bearing lubrication and package bottom. Test with your actual product before committing to a fixed installation angle.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is the weight limit for a gravity roller conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Capacity is rated per roller and per frame. Omni Metalcraft lists 94 lbs per roller for 1.4" rollers, 269 lbs for 1.9", and 645 lbs for 2.5". Product weight is shared by the rollers under it. Check the frame rating at your support spacing too. For heavy loads that need controlled movement, consider <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyors</a> for positive chain drive.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How much does gravity roller conveyor cost?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Gravity roller conveyor is usually the lowest cost type because it has no motor, drive, or controls. Cost factors include frame material (aluminum, galvanized steel, or stainless steel), roller diameter and gauge, bearing type, overall width, and length. Stainless steel construction for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food-grade applications</a> costs significantly more. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for a full breakdown.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What roller spacing should I use?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>At least three rollers should always be under the product. Divide your smallest product length by 3 and round down to the nearest roller center the manufacturer offers. A 12-inch case needs 4-inch or closer spacing. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor hub page</a> for more details on pitch selection.</p>

</div>
</div>
